 ## Overview
-This method stabilizes the projection of 3D Gaussians into screen space by applying a low-pass filter to the 2D covariance matrix, ensuring that each Gaussian spans at least the width of a single pixel to reduce aliasing artifacts [S2, unofficial]. It computes the inverse covariance (conic) matrix and a scalar rescale factor that preserves the integrated density of the Gaussian after filtering [S1, unofficial].
+This method computes the screen-space conic matrix (inverse covariance) and a rescale factor for a 2D Gaussian, applying a low-pass filter to the covariance matrix to ensure a minimum pixel coverage [S1, S2, unofficial]. It modifies the screen-space 2D Gaussian sizes to ensure they span the width of a single pixel, thereby reducing aliasing artifacts during rasterization [S2, unofficial]. It differs from its sibling [2D Covariance Correction via Linear Regression](9912-2d-covariance-correction-via-linear-regression.md) by using a fixed additive diagonal bias rather than learned regression coefficients to enforce the minimum size constraint [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:**
-- $\Sigma_n^{2D}$: The 2D screen-space covariance matrix for the $n$-th Gaussian [S2, unofficial].
-- $s$: The low-pass filter strength hyper-parameter. Default value is $0.3$ [S2, unofficial].
-
-**Outputs:**
-- `conic_and_rescale`: A 4-component vector containing the upper-triangular elements of the inverse covariance matrix and the rescale factor: $[ (\Sigma_n^{2D} + s\mathbf{I})^{-1}_{0,0}, (\Sigma_n^{2D} + s\mathbf{I})^{-1}_{0,1}, (\Sigma_n^{2D} + s\mathbf{I})^{-1}_{1,1}, \text{rescale} ]$ [S1, unofficial].
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
+*   **Input:** A 2x2 symmetric positive semi-definite covariance matrix $\Sigma^{2D}$ representing the projected 2D Gaussian in screen space.
+*   **Output:** A 4-component vector `conic_and_rescale` containing the elements of the inverse covariance matrix (conic matrix) and a scalar rescale factor. Specifically, `[inv_cov[0,0], inv_cov[0,1], inv_cov[1,1], rescale]`. Note that the conic matrix is symmetric, so `inv_cov[0,1]` equals `inv_cov[1,0]`.
 
 **Data Structures:**
-- $\Sigma_n^{2D}$: A $2 \times 2$ symmetric matrix $\begin{pmatrix} \Sigma_{00} & \Sigma_{01} \\ \Sigma_{01} & \Sigma_{11} \end{pmatrix}$ [S2, unofficial].
-- `conic_and_rescale`: A `vec4` (or array of 4 floats) [S1, unofficial].
+*   `gaussian_covariance`: A 2x2 matrix.
+*   `conic_and_rescale`: A 4-element vector (or array).
+
+**Parameters:**
+*   `s` (or `0.3`): The low-pass filter strength added to the diagonal elements. The default value is 0.3 [S1, S2, unofficial]. This value is set during training to ensure the 2D Gaussian's size spans the width of a single pixel [S2, unofficial].
 
 **Steps:**
-1. Compute the determinant of the original covariance matrix:
-   $$ \det_{\text{pre}} = \det(\Sigma_n^{2D}) = \Sigma_{00}\Sigma_{11} - \Sigma_{01}^2 $$
-   [S1, unofficial]
-2. Apply the low-pass filter by adding the scalar $s$ to the diagonal elements of the covariance matrix:
-   $$ \Sigma_{\text{filtered}} = \Sigma_n^{2D} + s\mathbf{I} = \begin{pmatrix} \Sigma_{00} + s & \Sigma_{01} \\ \Sigma_{01} & \Sigma_{11} + s \end{pmatrix} $$
-   [S1, S2, unofficial]
-3. Compute the determinant of the filtered covariance matrix:
-   $$ \det_{\text{post}} = \det(\Sigma_{\text{filtered}}) = (\Sigma_{00} + s)(\Sigma_{11} + s) - \Sigma_{01}^2 $$
-   [S1, unofficial]
-4. Compute the rescale factor to preserve the integral of the Gaussian density:
-   $$ \text{rescale} = \sqrt{\max(0.0, \frac{\det_{\text{pre}}}{\det_{\text{post}}})} $$
-   [S1, unofficial]
-5. Compute the inverse of the filtered covariance matrix (the conic matrix) analytically for the $2 \times 2$ case:
-   $$ \Sigma_{\text{filtered}}^{-1} = \frac{1}{\det_{\text{post}}} \begin{pmatrix} \Sigma_{11} + s & -\Sigma_{01} \\ -\Sigma_{01} & \Sigma_{00} + s \end{pmatrix} $$
-   [S1, unofficial]
-6. Pack the result into the output vector:
-   $$ \text{conic\_and\_rescale} = [\Sigma_{\text{filtered}}^{-1}[0,0], \Sigma_{\text{filtered}}^{-1}[0,1], \Sigma_{\text{filtered}}^{-1}[1,1], \text{rescale}] $$
-   [S1, unofficial]
+1.  Compute the determinant of the input covariance matrix:
+    $$ \text{det\_cov\_prefilter} = \det(\Sigma^{2D}) $$
+2.  Apply the low-pass filter by adding the scalar `s` to the diagonal elements of the covariance matrix:
+    $$ \Sigma^{2D}_{\text{filtered}} = \Sigma^{2D} + s \cdot \mathbf{I} $$
+    Where $\mathbf{I}$ is the 2x2 identity matrix. Specifically:
+    $$ \Sigma^{2D}_{\text{filtered}}[0,0] = \Sigma^{2D}[0,0] + s $$
+    $$ \Sigma^{2D}_{\text{filtered}}[1,1] = \Sigma^{2D}[1,1] + s $$
+3.  Compute the determinant of the filtered covariance matrix:
+    $$ \text{det\_cov} = \det(\Sigma^{2D}_{\text{filtered}}) $$
+4.  Compute the rescale factor. This factor adjusts the opacity/weight of the Gaussian to account for the volume change caused by the low-pass filtering:
+    $$ \text{rescale} = \sqrt{\max(0.0, \frac{\text{det\_cov\_prefilter}}{\text{det\_cov}})} $$
+5.  Compute the inverse covariance (conic) matrix analytically for the 2x2 filtered matrix. For a 2x2 matrix $A = \begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \end{pmatrix}$, the inverse is $\frac{1}{\det(A)} \begin{pmatrix} d & -b \\ -c & a \end{pmatrix}$. Since the covariance is symmetric ($b=c$):
+    $$ \text{inv\_cov} = \frac{1}{\text{det\_cov}} \begin{pmatrix} \Sigma^{2D}_{\text{filtered}}[1,1] & -\Sigma^{2D}_{\text{filtered}}[0,1] \\ -\Sigma^{2D}_{\text{filtered}}[1,0] & \Sigma^{2D}_{\text{filtered}}[0,0] \end{pmatrix} $$
+6.  Return the result as a 4-element vector: `[inv_cov[0,0], inv_cov[0,1], inv_cov[1,1], rescale]`.
 
